                    coach_r2_cohesiontrap:  `<strong>You kept the team almost entirely European.</strong> Cohesive, fast-deciding, proven — and entirely blind to Asia. You are entering Japan and Korea led by people who have never operated there, against a local competitor who has. What diversity was non-negotiable here?`,
                    coach_r2_overload:      `<strong>You replaced too much of the team at once.</strong> Maximum diversity, instantly — and you blew up the cohesion that lets a team decide fast. Strangers from several cultures, no shared history, no decision norms: that is a faultline, not a team, against a 12-month clock.`,
                    coach_r2_balanced:      `<strong>That is a balanced, fundable slate.</strong> A measured number of Asia-experienced hires plus an internal move or promotion, a protected European core, and screened fit — you added the right diversity within 12 seats and CHF 18M. Now make it cohere in Round 3.`,
                    coach_r2_speed:         `<strong>Speed-to-seat without fit screening seeds faultlines.</strong> Filling seats fastest and sorting fit out later means strangers arrive with no onboarding into a team with a 12-month clock — cohesion pays the bill.`,
                    coach_r2_fit:           `<strong>Fit-only hiring quietly re-homogenises the team.</strong> Screening hard for Swiss head-office comfort filters out the very Asia-experienced leaders you came to add — you bought seats, not capability or diversity.`,
                    coach_r2_mixed:         `<strong>A workable but unbalanced slate.</strong> Watch the trade-off you just made: too few Asia hires leaves you Europe-blind, too many without a continuity core fractures cohesion. Defend the balance you chose.`,
                    coach_r3_two:           `<strong>Two-first matches team to strategy.</strong> Launching Japan and Korea well inside 12 months, then India and SE Asia in year 2–3, fits the bandwidth a newly assembled team actually has. The sequence follows the team you built.`,
                    coach_r3_one:           `<strong>One-at-a-time is safe but cedes the region.</strong> Perfecting Japan while deferring the other three indefinitely misses the window before the competitor consolidates — the board wanted two entries inside 12 months, not one flawless one.`,
                    coach_r3_all:           `<strong>All four at once under-leads every entry.</strong> Your budget funds two entries well or four badly, and a freshly assembled team has the bandwidth for two. Spread across four, every entry is under-led and you miss the 12-month window on all of them.`,
                    coach_r3_matched:       `<strong>Profile-to-market matching turns diversity into capability.</strong> Your Asia-experienced hires lead the entries that need them, with the two-first sequence. That is how a diverse appointment list becomes execution, not a wall chart.`,
                    coach_r3_seniority:     `<strong>Leading by seniority wastes the diversity you bought.</strong> Putting the longest-serving Europe veterans in charge of Asia entries — regardless of Asia experience — recreates the Europe-only blind spot you spent CHF millions to fix.`,
                    coach_r3_single:        `<strong>One Asia hire leading everything is a single point of failure.</strong> Concentrating the scarce experience in one person means every entry waits on one diary, and if they leave, the whole strategy goes with them.`,
                    coach_r3_nointegration: `<strong>Diversity you do not integrate is just disagreement with better CVs.</strong> With no onboarding, shared norms or faultline repair, the European core and the Asia hires run parallel organisations and decisions stall in committee. Integration is the boundary condition.`,
                    coach_r4_right:         `<strong>An honest, accurate read.</strong> You added the right diversity and integrated it — capturing the perspective and market-knowledge gains while containing the process cost. That is precisely the balance Ruigrok's research says converts diversity into performance.`,
                    coach_r4_toolittle:     `<strong>Naming it honestly is the first recalibration.</strong> Too little diversity left the team Europe-blind and short of Asia market knowledge — the entries were led by people learning the region on the firm's time. Add the missing capability deliberately now.`,
                    coach_r4_toomuch:       `<strong>Too much too fast is a real failure mode.</strong> The cohesion fractured and decisions stalled in committee — you captured perspective range but paid more process cost than a 12-month clock could absorb. Recalibrate toward integration, not more swaps.`,
                    coach_r4_freeze:        `<strong>Freezing locks in whatever you have, good or bad.</strong> If the team is still fracturing, 'let everyone settle' just lets the faultlines harden. Deliberate integration and the next capability beats standing still.`,
                    coach_r4_reshuffle:     `<strong>Reshuffling again to chase the index is the vanity trap.</strong> More churn means more strangers and more lost cohesion — for a prettier number, not more Asia revenue. The point was never the index.`,
                    coach_r4_revenue:       `<strong>That is the board-ready answer.</strong> Your TMT design served expansion performance — here is the APAC revenue trajectory it delivered, integration and all. Composition in service of internationalization, exactly the thesis you were hired on.`,
                    coach_r4_index:         `<strong>The index is beautiful; the market never rewarded it.</strong> Five nationalities, board-presentation-ready — but if entries slipped and ROI lagged, a diverse team that does not execute is a governance trophy, not a strategy.`,
                    coach_r4_harmony:       `<strong>Optimising for harmony is the cohesion trap, restated.</strong> Keeping the team comfortable and avoiding disruption is how you forfeit the Asia capability the CHF 690M target runs on. Comfort was never the brief.`,

